      "slug": "cwe-78-os-command-injection",
      "tag": "cwe-78",
      "short": "OS Command Injection",
      "desc": "Shell escapes via unsanitized input, and why allowlists matter.",
      "title": "CWE-78: OS Command Injection",
      "sub": "When a web form becomes a root shell",
      "category": "Threats & Attacks",
      "cover": "covers/command.webp",
      "toc": [
        {
          "id": "sec-definition-goal",
          "text": "Definition &amp; Goal"
        },
        {
          "id": "sec-why-it-s-so-dangerous",
          "text": "Why It’s So Dangerous"
        },
        {
          "id": "sec-how-it-works",
          "text": "How It Works"
        }
      ],
      "quote": "One semicolon. One pipe. And the server becomes yours.",
      "review": [
        {
          "q": "What causes OS Command Injection?",
          "a": "User input passed to a shell without sanitization, enabling escape via ; | &amp;&amp; $()"
        },
        {
          "q": "Which characters enable injection?",
          "a": "Semicolons (;), pipes (|), double ampersands (&amp;&amp;), subshell $()."
        },
        {
          "q": "Why is this critical?",
          "a": "Arbitrary OS command execution; root access means full system compromise."
        },
        {
          "q": "How do you prevent it?",
          "a": "Never pass raw input to shells. Use parameterized APIs and allowlists."
        }
      ],
      "terms": [
